# ============================================================
# How it works
# ============================================================
#
# When a PR modifies /proxy/main.go:
#   1. GitHub reads CODEOWNERS.
#   2. It finds the last matching pattern for the path.
#   3. It assigns @gasciljh as a reviewer automatically.
#   4. The user does not need to request a review manually.
#
# When a PR modifies an undefined file (e.g. /newfile.txt):
#   → matched by `*` (first line) → @gasciljh.
#
# When a PR modifies /proxy/main.go and /docs/FAQ.md:
#   → all matching owners are assigned (@gasciljh only, currently).
#
# When a PR adds a new ADR in /docs/adr/:
#   → matched by `/docs/adr/` → @gasciljh (mandatory review —
#     ADRs are immutable once accepted).
#
# When a PR modifies /scripts/release-patch.sh:
#   → matched by `/scripts/` and `/scripts/release-patch.sh`.
#     The more specific pattern wins — but both point to @gasciljh.
#
# ============================================================
# Benefits
# ============================================================
#
# ✅ Faster review: the reviewer is assigned immediately.
# ✅ No oversight: every PR is automatically reviewed.
# ✅ Fine-grained: each file can have a different reviewer.
# ✅ Team-friendly: supports team handles like @org/team-name.
# ✅ GitHub standard: officially supported feature.
# ✅ ADR-aware: architectural decisions trigger the right reviewer.
#
